This directory contains examples that use SALT-FM to instrument C, C++, and
Fortran programs. SALT-FM is an LLVM-based source instrumentor. It parses each
source with Clang or Flang and writes an instrumented copy. With
-optSaltInst, the TAU compiler scripts (tau_cc.sh, tau_cxx.sh, and tau_f90.sh)
run SALT-FM and then compile its output.

To install SALT-FM with Spack:
$ ./install_salt.sh
$ spack load saltfm

install_salt.sh runs "spack install saltfm". If Spack already has an LLVM
with Clang and Flang (see "spack compiler find" and "spack external find
llvm"), SALT-FM uses it and Spack does not build LLVM.

Each example is a matrix multiply program with a Makefile and a run.sh script
that builds the program, runs it, and calls pprof. run.sh sets TAU_CALLPATH=1
and TAU_CALLPATH_DEPTH=100, so paraprof can show a call graph (Windows >
Thread > Call Graph). Set TAU_MAKEFILE to the TAU configuration that the
example needs and run run.sh in its directory:
$ export TAU_MAKEFILE=<taudir>/x86_64/lib/Makefile.tau-clang
$ cd serial/c
$ ./run.sh

serial          - C, C++, and Fortran. The Fortran example uses a module, a
                  submodule, and a selective instrumentation file.

openmp          - C, C++, and Fortran with OpenMP. TAU must be configured with
                  the -openmp and -ompt options.

mpi             - C, C++, and Fortran with MPI. TAU must be configured with
                  the -mpi option. There are two Fortran examples:
                  f90_mpif uses include "mpif.h", and f90_mpi_module uses the
                  mpi module.

direct          - Runs saltfm by hand and compiles its output with the flags
                  from the TAU Makefile, without the TAU compiler scripts.

These examples were tested with TAU configured with:
$ ./configure -cc=clang -c++=clang++ -fortran=flang
and with -openmp -ompt or -mpi added, as each directory's README shows.
